    id: "pronouns",
    title: "Subject pronouns: ‹io›, ‹tu›, ‹Lei›, ‹voi›",
    blurb: "Know the words for I, you, he, she, we and they, when to use ‹Lei› and ‹voi›, and when to leave them out.",
    deck: "About me",
    body: `
<p>The subject is who the sentence is about. English has <i>I, you, he, she, we, they</i>. Italian has a few more, because it splits "you" three ways.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Italian</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">io</td><td>I</td></tr><tr><td class="k">tu</td><td>you (a friend)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">lui, lei</td><td>he, she</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Lei</td><td>you (a stranger)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">noi</td><td>we</td></tr><tr><td class="k">voi</td><td>you all</td></tr><tr><td class="k">loro</td><td>they</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>The verb changes with the subject</h3>
<p>In English, "how are you" and "how is she" barely change. In Italian each subject has its own verb ending. You already know <b>come stai?</b> and <b>come sta?</b>. Here is the whole verb for "how are you", the verb <b>stare</b>:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Subject</th><th>‹stare›</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">io</td><td class="k">sto</td><td>I am</td></tr><tr><td class="k">tu</td><td class="k">stai</td><td>you are (a friend)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">lui, lei, Lei</td><td class="k">sta</td><td>he is, she is, you are (a stranger)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">noi</td><td class="k">stiamo</td><td>we are</td></tr><tr><td class="k">voi</td><td class="k">state</td><td>you all are</td></tr><tr><td class="k">loro</td><td class="k">stanno</td><td>they are</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>‹Lei›: the formal "you"</h3>
<p><b>Lei</b> with a capital L is how you say "you" to a stranger, and it takes the same verb as <b>lei</b>, "she". So <b>Lei come sta?</b> is "How are you?" to a stranger, and <b>E lei come sta?</b> with a small l is "And how is she?". In speech, the situation tells you which.</p>
{{ex:Signora, Lei come sta?|see-NYOH-rah lay KOH-meh stah|Ma'am, how are you?}}
{{ex:E Lei?|eh lay|And you? (to a stranger)}}
<h3>‹Voi›: more than one "you"</h3>
<p>English uses "you" for one person or many. Italian uses <b>voi</b> for any group, friends or strangers. In very formal service you may hear <b>Loro</b> for a group of guests; you don't need to say it yourself.</p>
{{ex:Ragazzi, come state?|rah-GAHT-tsee KOH-meh STAH-teh|Hey guys, how are you all?}}
{{ex:Marco e Chiara stanno bene.|MAHR-koh eh KYAH-rah STAHN-noh BEH-neh|Marco and Chiara are fine.}}
<h3>Leave the pronoun out</h3>
<p>English always needs "I" or "we". In Italian the verb ending already tells you who, so <b>sto</b> alone means "I am". Italians usually drop <b>io</b>, <b>tu</b> and <b>noi</b>, and keep a pronoun for contrast or to make clear who is meant.</p>
{{ex:Sto bene, grazie.|stoh BEH-neh GRAH-tsyeh|I'm fine, thanks.}}
{{ex:Stiamo bene. E voi?|STYAH-moh BEH-neh eh voy|We're fine. And you all?}}
<p>That is also why <b>Mi chiamo Emma</b> needs no <b>io</b> in front.</p>
<div class="tip"><strong>Most common mistake:</strong> keeping <b>io</b> in every sentence, as English does. <b>Io sto bene, io mi chiamo Emma</b> → <b>Sto bene, mi chiamo Emma</b>.</div>
`,
