    id: "adjectives",
    title: "Adjectives: agreement and position",
    blurb: "Describe clothes and people with adjectives that come after the noun and agree with it.",
    deck: "Shopping and clothes",
    body: `
<p>English adjectives never change and come before the noun: "the red shirt", "the red shoes". Italian adjectives do two things differently. Most come after the noun, and they change their ending to agree with it.</p>
<h3>After the noun</h3>
<p>Say the thing first, then describe it: <b>la camicia bianca</b>, literally "the shirt white". Colors and nationalities always go after the noun.</p>
{{ex:la camicia bianca|lah kah-MEE-chah BYAHN-kah|the white shirt}}
<h3>Four forms</h3>
<p>An adjective ending in <b>-o</b> has four forms: <b>-o</b> and <b>-a</b> for masculine and feminine, <b>-i</b> and <b>-e</b> for the plurals. Check the noun first, then pick the ending. Follow the gender, not the letters: <b>il maglione</b> (the sweater) ends in <b>-e</b> but is masculine, so it's <b>il maglione nero</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>One</th><th>More than one</th></tr><tr><td>Masculine</td><td class="k">il maglione nero</td><td class="k">i pantaloni neri</td></tr><tr><td>Feminine</td><td class="k">la gonna nera</td><td class="k">le scarpe nere</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:le scarpe nere|leh SKAHR-peh NEH-reh|the black shoes}}
{{ex:i pantaloni neri|ee pahn-tah-LOH-nee NEH-ree|the black pants}}
<p>Adjectives in <b>-co</b> and <b>-ca</b> add an <b>h</b> in the plural to keep the hard sound: <b>bianco</b>, <b>bianchi</b>; <b>bianca</b>, <b>bianche</b>.</p>
<h3>Two forms</h3>
<p>Adjectives ending in <b>-e</b> are the same for masculine and feminine. They have one form for one thing and <b>-i</b> for more than one.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>One</th><th>More than one</th></tr><tr><td class="k">la borsa verde</td><td class="k">le borse verdi</td></tr><tr><td class="k">il cappotto verde</td><td class="k">i cappotti verdi</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>After ‹essere›</h3>
<p>The adjective agrees even after the verb: it looks back at the subject. A mixed group of men and women takes the masculine plural.</p>
{{ex:La giacca è rossa.|lah JAHK-kah eh ROHS-sah|The jacket is red.}}
{{ex:Le borse sono verdi.|leh BOHR-seh SOH-noh VEHR-dee|The bags are green.}}
{{ex:Giulia e Marco sono alti.|JOO-lyah eh MAHR-koh SOH-noh AHL-tee|Giulia and Marco are tall.}}
<h3>A few go first</h3>
<p>Some very common adjectives usually go before the noun: <b>bello</b> (beautiful), <b>buono</b> (good), <b>piccolo</b> (small). Before the noun <b>bello</b> and <b>buono</b> can shorten, as in <b>buongiorno</b>; you only need to recognize this for now.</p>
{{ex:un bel vestito|oon behl vehs-TEE-toh|a nice dress}}
<p>Useful colors: <b>rosso</b> (red), <b>bianco</b> (white), <b>nero</b> (black), <b>giallo</b> (yellow), <b>verde</b> (green). For prices: <b>caro</b> (expensive).</p>
<div class="tip"><strong>Most common mistake:</strong> keeping the adjective masculine. <b>la camicia bianco</b> → <b>la camicia bianca</b>.</div>
`,
